        <section class="detail-section" id="origin">
            <div class="activity-wrap">
                <header class="detail-intro trust-intro">
                    <span class="activity-eyebrow">Why the Trust Began</span>
                    <h2>Education was available in principle. For many girls, it remained unreachable in practice.</h2>
                    <p>Across western Rajasthan, financial hardship, preference for sons and inadequate learning conditions repeatedly interrupted girls’ education. Bhaiyaji saw that paying a fee could prevent one immediate loss—but meaningful empowerment required education to be joined with character and the ability to stand independently.</p>
                </header>

                <article class="detail-story trust-origin-story">
                    <figure class="detail-photo trust-photo-wide">
                        <img src="assets/images/girls2.png" alt="A group of smiling schoolgirls standing together" loading="eager">
                        <figcaption>Every girl carries ability, aspiration and a rightful place in education.</figcaption>
                    </figure>
                    <div class="detail-story-copy">
                        <span class="activity-eyebrow">A Conviction Becomes a Structure</span>
                        <h2>Support that protects both education and self-respect</h2>
                        <p>The Trust was established on 10 June 1996 under Bhaiyaji’s guiding vision, with Maa Basanti helping translate that conviction into organised service. Its purpose was not to create one more school, but to reach deserving girls across many schools and respond wherever financial difficulty threatened continuity.</p>
                        <p>From the beginning, assistance was offered as a rightful opportunity rather than an act of pity. This distinction remains central: a student should be able to receive help with confidence, concentrate on learning and imagine a future larger than her present circumstances.</p>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <div class="detail-reflection">The Trust does not see a beneficiary waiting to be rescued. It sees a capable young person whose path must not be closed by circumstances.</div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="detail-band trust-principles" id="principles">
            <div class="activity-wrap">
                <header class="detail-band-header">
                    <span class="activity-eyebrow">The Complete Educational Path</span>
                    <h2>Three foundations, held together throughout a student’s journey</h2>
                </header>
                <div class="detail-card-grid">
                    <article class="detail-card">
                        <span>01 · Opportunity</span>
                        <h3>Academic access</h3>
                        <p>Scholarships, fees, books and essential learning support help ensure that a genuine financial barrier does not become the end of school education.</p>
                    </article>
                    <article class="detail-card">
                        <span>02 · Inner formation</span>
                        <h3>Sanskar</h3>
                        <p>Values, discernment, discipline, gratitude and compassion help students understand not only what they can achieve, but how they wish to live.</p>
                    </article>
                    <article class="detail-card">
                        <span>03 · A dignified future</span>
                        <h3>Self-reliance</h3>
                        <p>Mentoring, exposure, confidence and practical guidance prepare girls to make informed choices and participate in society without dependence or fear.</p>
                    </article>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="trust-process-section" id="selection">
            <div class="activity-wrap">
                <header class="detail-intro">
                    <span class="activity-eyebrow">How Support Begins</span>
                    <h2>Need is understood carefully, and assistance moves through a transparent path</h2>
                    <p>Selection is based on genuine financial need—not marks, caste, community, religion or personal influence. Schools play an important role because teachers are often the first to recognise when a student is at risk of leaving education.</p>
                </header>

                <ol class="trust-process" aria-label="School scholarship selection process">
                    <li>
                        <span class="trust-process-number">01</span>
                        <div><strong>A teacher recognises the difficulty</strong><p>The class teacher observes when family circumstances are placing a girl’s attendance, fees or continued schooling at risk.</p></div>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="trust-process-number">02</span>
                        <div><strong>The school verifies the situation</strong><p>The Principal reviews the need and formally recommends the student, creating a responsible institutional starting point.</p></div>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="trust-process-number">03</span>
                        <div><strong>The Trust considers the real requirement</strong><p>Support is aligned with the student’s educational circumstances rather than determined through rank, social identity or favour.</p></div>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="trust-process-number">04</span>
                        <div><strong>Assistance reaches education directly</strong><p>Fees and learning support are routed through the institution wherever applicable, maintaining accountability and protecting the student’s dignity.</p></div>
                    </li>
                </ol>

                <aside class="trust-eligibility" aria-label="Selection principles">
                    <div><span>Who it serves</span><strong>School-going girls facing genuine financial hardship</strong></div>
                    <div><span>What does not decide support</span><strong>Marks alone, caste, community, religion or personal influence</strong></div>
                    <div><span>How a family can begin</span><strong>Speak openly with the student’s class teacher or Principal</strong></div>
                </aside>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="detail-section trust-everyday" id="everyday-work">
            <div class="activity-wrap">
                <header class="detail-intro">
                    <span class="activity-eyebrow">Beyond the Scholarship</span>
                    <h2>The everyday work is a continuing relationship—not a once-a-year transaction</h2>
                    <p>Financial support removes an immediate barrier. Regular contact, listening and guidance help a student build the confidence and practical understanding needed to use that opportunity well.</p>
                </header>

                <article class="detail-story">
                    <figure class="detail-photo">
                        <img src="assets/images/sanskar_classes.jpg" alt="Students listening attentively during a Sanskar gathering at Manidweep" loading="lazy">
                        <figcaption>Students return to a familiar space where questions, difficulties and aspirations can be discussed without judgement.</figcaption>
                    </figure>
                    <div class="detail-story-copy">
                        <span class="activity-eyebrow">Sunday at Manidweep</span>
                        <h2>Guidance that stays present as life changes</h2>
                        <p>During the active class months, Sunday Sanskar and personality-development sessions bring students into sustained contact with mentors. Conversations cover study habits, time management, examination preparation, emotional pressures, health, relationships, rights and responsibilities.</p>
                        <p>Qualified volunteers and invited speakers help widen students’ awareness of educational and professional possibilities. When a student reaches a transition—another class, college, vocational learning or employment—the relationship makes it easier to seek direction rather than face the decision alone.</p>
                        <a class="practice-source" href="sunday-sanskar-classes.html">Explore Sunday Sanskar Classes</a>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <div class="trust-support-grid" aria-label="Forms of support">
                    <article><span>Financial continuity</span><p>School fees, scholarships and essential learning needs are considered according to the student’s circumstances.</p></article>
                    <article><span>Academic guidance</span><p>Study practices, examination methods and individual difficulties are addressed through conversation and mentorship.</p></article>
                    <article><span>Confidence &amp; expression</span><p>Students are encouraged to ask questions, speak publicly, make decisions and recognise their own capabilities.</p></article>
                    <article><span>Values &amp; inner balance</span><p>Sanskar, Yoga, Pranayama, meditation and reflection connect intellectual growth with emotional steadiness.</p></article>
                    <article><span>Career awareness</span><p>Exposure to professionals and educational pathways helps aspirations become more informed and achievable.</p></article>
                    <article><span>The circle of service</span><p>Students who become self-reliant are encouraged to strengthen the same opportunity for another girl.</p></article>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

